/*  doWhileNameT -- THE NEW PARSE ROAD CRASHES WHEN A DO's WHILE EXPRESSION IS NOT A
    BARE LITERAL. Born RED on purpose, 2026-09-20, Clay's SEQ 171 step 1.

    ⚠ THE ROW IS A PIN, NOT A DIAGNOSIS. What is asserted is that the run reaches its
    foot. It does not today: the target drive dies at EXC_BAD_ACCESS in aCTionTokenXP,
    reading a null ANYorNum label. The row goes green when the label channel lands.

    ⚠ THE CONTROL ROW IS NOT DECORATION AND IT IS WHY THIS FIXTURE IS ONE FILE. Both
    drives run under THE SAME parser of DO -- one generation, two drive strings -- so a
    red target cannot be blamed on generation. The control returns first and prints a
    marker, so a run that dies before reaching the target fails the control row too and
    the pin cannot be read as satisfied by a fixture that never got started.

    ⚠ THERE IS NO OLD-ROAD ORACLE FOR THE TARGET AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT AN
    OMISSION. Driven with no parser at all, the same statement refuses at the bump --
    the field it names was never assigned -- prints nothing, and exits zero. So the old
    road has no correct answer here to be compared against, and the certificate's other
    half applies instead: name the rule where the new road now stops.  */
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search GroupFields UnitTests Utilities list;

cerr "DW-0 parser generates for DO":;
parser(DO);

cerr "DW-1 control -- a literal while expression":;
DO("do print 1; while 0;");
cerr "DW-1 CONTROL RETURNED":;

cerr "DW-2 target -- a NAME in the while expression":;
DO("do print ++result; while result < 2;");
cerr "DW-2 TARGET RETURNED":;

cerr "DOWHILENAME SENTINEL":;
stop();

This text is past stop(); the parse halts there and nothing below is read.

/*****************************************************************************
                    What DW-2 Is Pinning
*****************************************************************************/
    The crash, with frames, measured 2026-09-20 on a bare binary, canary 335:

        EXC_BAD_ACCESS (code=1, address=0x0)
        aCTionTokenXP(xpress=0x...) at GroupRules.mm:1265
            if ( isGROUP(ANYtoken->groupBody->flags.data) )

    Bear-trap 36 applies and the line that died is not the line that read null. The
    PRODUCER is GroupRules.mm:1249, ruleActions.rtn:987:

        GroupItem ANYtoken = xpress["ANYorNum"];

    xpress is NOT null -- it carries a real address. What is missing is its ANYorNum
    member, which is Tony's "TokenXP receives xpress with no list" said from the other
    end. The sub-term's label never attached, so the seat reads the slot as null and
    dereferences it two lines later.

    The generated body TokenXP now carries is

        return UnaryOPS() && ANYorNum() && InvokeArg();

    against a grammar line whose first and third terms are OPTIONAL. The emitted body
    carries no modifiers -- the known F-89 seam -- so two optional terms are conjuncts
    that must succeed.

/*****************************************************************************
                    The Ladder That Named The Variable
*****************************************************************************/
    One run each, same parser of DO, the drive string the only thing changed, alarm
    at sixty seconds. Exit 139 is the crash; exit 0 reached the sentinel.

        A   do print 1; while 0;                 exit 0
        B   do print 1; while 1 less 0;          exit 0
        C   do print 1; while result less 2;     exit 139
        D   do print ++result; while 0;          exit 0
        E   do print ++result; while result less 2;   exit 139
        F   do print 1; while not result less 2; exit 139
        G   do print 1; while result;            exit 139
        H   do print 1; while not 1;             exit 139
        I   do print 1; while not result;        exit 139

    D is the row that matters most and it is easy to miss: the bump and the name are
    fine in the STATEMENT BODY. Only the while EXPRESSION kills it. So the variable is
    not the bump, not the comparison, and not the name as such -- A and B pass with a
    comparison of literals, and every row that routes the while expression through
    TokenXP dies.

    ⚠ H IS A COUNTEREXAMPLE AND IT IS RECORDED BECAUSE IT KILLS THE TIDIEST READING.
    The obvious story is that an ABSENT optional unary returns falsy, short-circuits
    the conjunction, and leaves ANYorNum unasked. H supplies a unary on a literal --
    present, not absent -- and still dies. So a short-circuit at an absent UnaryOPS
    cannot be the whole cause. What that positive cause IS has not been measured here,
    and nothing in this file claims one.
